WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.400
Today I'd like to share with you three fake&nbsp;
proofs, in increasing order of subtlety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.120
and then discuss what each one of&nbsp;
them has to tell us about math.
&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.200
The first proof is for a formula&nbsp;
for the surface area of a sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.080
and the way that it starts is to subdivide that&nbsp;
sphere into vertical slices, the way you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.040
chop up an orange or paint a beach ball.
We then unravel all of those wedge slices from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.600
the northern hemisphere so that they poke up&nbsp;
like this, and then symmetrically unravel all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.480
of those from the southern hemisphere below,&nbsp;
and now interlace those pieces to get a shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.160
whose area we want to figure out. The base of this&nbsp;
shape came from the circumference of the sphere;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.280
it's an unraveled equator. So its length&nbsp;
is 2π times the radius of the sphere.
&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.000
And then the other side of this shape came&nbsp;
from the height of one of these wedges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.440
which is a quarter of a walk around the sphere,&nbsp;
and so it has a length of π/2 times R. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.280
idea is that this is only an approximation,&nbsp;
the edges might not be perfectly straight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.040
but if we think of the limit as we do&nbsp;
finer and finer slices of the sphere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.360
this shape whose area we want to know&nbsp;
gets closer to being a perfect rectangle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:14.000
one whose area will be π/2 R times 2π R. Or&nbsp;
in other words, π squared times R squared.
&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.840
The proof is elegant. It translates a hard problem&nbsp;
into a situation that's easier to understand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.080
It has that element of surprise while&nbsp;
still being intuitive. It's only fault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.920
really, is that it's completely wrong. The true&nbsp;
surface area of a sphere is 4π R squared.
&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.600
I originally saw this example thanks&nbsp;
to Henry Reich, and to be fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.360
it's not necessarily inconsistent with the 4π R&nbsp;
squared formula, just so long as π is equal to 4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.480
For the next proof I'd like to show you a simple&nbsp;
argument for the fact that π is equal to four.
&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.800
We start off with a circle, say with radius 1, and&nbsp;
we ask how can we figure out its circumference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.920
After all π is by definition the ratio of this&nbsp;
circumference to the diameter of the circle. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.960
start off by drawing the square whose side lengths&nbsp;
are all tangent to that circle. It's not too hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.840
to see that the perimeter of the square is 8.
Then, and some of you may have seen this before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.480
it's a kind of classic argument, the argument&nbsp;
proceeds by producing a sequence of curves, all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.760
of whom also have this perimeter of 8, but which&nbsp;
more and more closely approximate the circle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.920
But the full nuance of this example is&nbsp;
not always emphasized. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.680
just to make things crystal clear, the way&nbsp;
each of these iterations works is to fold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.760
in each of the corners of the previous shape&nbsp;
so that they just barely kiss the circle.
&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.080
You can take a moment to convince yourself&nbsp;
that in each region where a fold happened, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.560
perimeter doesn't change. For example in the upper&nbsp;
right here, instead of walking up and then left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.520
the new curve goes left and then up. And something&nbsp;
similar is true at all of the folds of all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.160
different iterations, wherever the previous&nbsp;
iteration went direction A then direction B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.280
the new iteration goes direction B then&nbsp;
direction A. But no length is lost or gained.
&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.600
Some of you might say, well obviously this isn't&nbsp;
going to give the true perimeter of the circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.280
because no matter how many iterations you&nbsp;
do, when you zoom in it remains jagged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.440
It's not a smooth curve, you're taking these&nbsp;
very inefficient steps along the circle.
&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.520
While that is true and ultimately the reason&nbsp;
things are wrong, if you want to appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.760
the lesson this example is teaching us, the&nbsp;
claim of the example is not that any one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.720
these approximations equals the curve. It's that&nbsp;
the limit of all of the approximations equals our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.280
circle, and to appreciate the lesson that this&nbsp;
example teaches us, it's worth taking a moment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.000
be a little more mathematically precise about what&nbsp;
I mean by the limit of a sequence of curves.
&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.520
Let's say we describe the very first shape,&nbsp;
this square, as a parametric function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.600
Something that has an input t,&nbsp;
and it outputs a point in 2d space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.080
so that as t ranges from 0 to 1, it traces that&nbsp;
square. I'll call that function c0. And likewise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.040
we can parametrize the next iteration with&nbsp;
a function I'll call c1. As the parameter t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.240
ranges from zero up to one, the output of&nbsp;
this function traces along that curve. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.200
is just so that we can think of these&nbsp;
shapes as instead being functions.
&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.080
Now i want you to consider a particular value of&nbsp;
t, maybe 0.2, and then consider the sequence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.920
points that you get by evaluating the sequence&nbsp;
of functions we have at this particular point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.840
Next I want you to consider the limit&nbsp;
as n approaches infinity of c_n of 0.2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:20.640
This limit is a well-defined point in 2d space,&nbsp;
in fact that point sits on the circle. And there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.320
nothing specific about 0.2, we could do this&nbsp;
limiting process for any input t, and so I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.640
define a new function that I'll call c_infinity,&nbsp;
which by definition at any input t is whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:38.160
this limiting value for all the curves is.
So here's the point: That limiting function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.280
c_infinity is the circle. It's not an&nbsp;
approximation of the circle, it's not some jagged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.560
version of the circle, it is the genuine smooth&nbsp;
circular curve whose perimeter we want to know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:55.360
And what's also true is that the limit of the&nbsp;
lengths of all of our curves really is eight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.920
because each individual curve really does&nbsp;
have a perimeter of eight. And there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.920
all sorts of examples throughout calculus&nbsp;
when we talk about approximating one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.080
we want to know as a limit of a bunch of&nbsp;
other things that are easier to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.920
so the question at the heart here is why exactly&nbsp;
is it not okay to do that in this example?
&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.480
Maybe at this point you step back and say, you&nbsp;
know it's just not enough for things to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.760
the same. This is why we need rigor, it's why we&nbsp;
need proofs, it's why, since the days of Euclid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.680
mathematicians have followed in his footsteps and&nbsp;
deduced truths step-by-step from axioms forward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.080
But for this last example I would like to do&nbsp;
something that doesn't lean as hard on visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:42.640
intuition, and instead give a Euclid-style proof&nbsp;
for the claim that all triangles are isosceles.
&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.720
The way this will work is we'll take any&nbsp;
particular triangle and make no assumptions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:52.400
it. I'll label its vertices A, B and C. And what&nbsp;
I would like to prove for you is that the side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:59.600
length AB is necessarily equal to the side length&nbsp;
AC. Now to be clear, the result is obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

