the slope of the edge of this red triangle works&nbsp;
out to be 7 divided by 3. Those numbers are close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:16.640
enough to look similar as slope but they allow for&nbsp;
this denting inward and the bulging outward.
&nbsp;

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.640
You have to be wary of lines that are made to&nbsp;
look straight when you haven't had explicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.240
confirmation that they actually are straight.
One quick added comment on the sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:31.280
The fundamental issue here is that the geometry&nbsp;
of a curved surface is fundamentally different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:36.320
from the geometry of flat space. The relevant&nbsp;
search term here would be gaussian curvature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.320
You can't flatten things out from a sphere&nbsp;
without losing geometric information.
&nbsp;

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:45.760
Now when you do see limiting arguments that&nbsp;
relate to little pieces on a sphere that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:50.960
somehow get flattened out and are reasoned through&nbsp;
there. Those only can work if the limiting pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:55.520
that you're talking about get smaller in both&nbsp;
directions. It's only when you zoom in close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.520 --> 00:15:01.600
a curved surface that it appears locally flat. The&nbsp;
issue with our orange wedge argument is that our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:06.560
pieces never got exposed to that local flatness&nbsp;
because they only got thin in one direction; they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:11.520
maintain the curvature in that other direction.
Now on the topic of the subtlety of limiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:17.040
arguments, let's turn back to our limit of jagged&nbsp;
curves that approaches the smooth circular curve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:22.160
As I said, the limiting curve really is a circle&nbsp;
and the limiting value for the length of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:28.880
approximations really is eight. Here the basic&nbsp;
issue is that there is no reason to expect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.920
the limit of the lengths of the curves is the same&nbsp;
as the length of the limits of the curves. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:39.440
in fact, this is a nice counter example to show&nbsp;
why that's not the case. The real point of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:44.400
example is not the fear that anyone is ever going&nbsp;
to believe that it shows that π is equal to 4.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:50.480
Instead, it shows why care is required in other&nbsp;
cases where people apply limiting arguments.
&nbsp;

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:55.600
For example, this happens all throughout calculus,&nbsp;
it is the heart of calculus. Where, say you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:01.120
to know the area under a given curve, the way we&nbsp;
typically think about it is to approximate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:05.600
with a set of rectangles, because those are&nbsp;
the things we know how to compute the areas of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:10.400
you just take the base times height in each&nbsp;
case. Now this is a very jagged approximation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:15.360
but the thought or the hope is that as you&nbsp;
take a finer and finer subdivision into thinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:20.160
and thinner rectangles the sums of those areas&nbsp;
approaches the thing we actually care about.
&nbsp;

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:24.880
If you want to make it rigorous you have&nbsp;
to be explicit about the error between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:30.080
these approximations and the true thing we care&nbsp;
about, the area under this curve. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:34.880
you might start your argument by saying that that&nbsp;
error has to be strictly less than the area of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:40.560
these red rectangles. Essentially, the deviation&nbsp;
between the curve and our approximating rectangles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:45.440
sits strictly inside that red region. And then,&nbsp;
what you would want to argue, is that in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.800
limiting process the cumulative area of all of&nbsp;
those red rectangles has to approach zero.
&nbsp;

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:01.280
Now as to the final example: our proof&nbsp;
that all triangles are isosceles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:04.480
Let me show you what it looks like if I'm&nbsp;
a little bit more careful about actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.080
constructing the angle bisector&nbsp;
rather than just eyeballing it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:12.960
When I do that the relevant intersection&nbsp;
point actually sits outside of the triangle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:16.640
And then from there, if I go through everything&nbsp;
that we did in the original argument drawing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:21.760
relevant perpendicular lines, all of that. Every&nbsp;
triangle that I claimed was congruent really is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.920
congruent. All of those were genuinely true and&nbsp;
the corresponding lengths of those triangles that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:30.880
I claimed were the same really are the same.&nbsp;
The one place where the proof breaks down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:38.000
is at the very end when I said that the full&nbsp;
side length AC was equal to AE plus EC. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:44.400
was only true under the hidden assumption that&nbsp;
that point E sat in between them, but in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:49.440
for many triangles that point would sit outside&nbsp;
of those two. It's pretty subtle isn't it?
&nbsp;

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:56.560
The point in all of this is that while visual&nbsp;
intuition is great and visual proofs often give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:00.560
you a nice way of elucidating what's&nbsp;
going on with otherwise opaque rigor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:06.240
Visual arguments and snazzy diagrams will&nbsp;
never obviate the need for critical thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:13.840
In math you cannot escape the need to look&nbsp;
out for hidden assumptions and edge cases.

